WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.686 --> 00:00:09.144
Marcus Bell: Morning, both. How's Thursday treating you?

2
00:00:09.371 --> 00:00:14.036
Dana Ortiz: Morning. Busy. We're heading into furnace season, so it's all hands.

3
00:00:15.354 --> 00:00:18.844
Priyanka Shah: Hi Marcus. I've got coffee, so I'm ready.

4
00:00:19.231 --> 00:00:23.624
Marcus Bell: Good. Before we get into anything, how are volumes looking?

5
00:00:24.897 --> 00:00:32.260
Priyanka Shah: Calls are up about 18 percent over September. Last week we were around 9,400 tracked calls across the 14 branches.

6
00:00:32.613 --> 00:00:37.138
Marcus Bell: That's a jump. Is that the paid search push for heating tune-ups?

7
00:00:38.063 --> 00:00:44.313
Priyanka Shah: Yeah, plus two new branches in Greenville and Gastonia came onto the tracking numbers this month.

8
00:00:44.936 --> 00:00:54.852
Dana Ortiz: And we've got a staffing thing on top of it. We're down two dispatchers in Charlotte, so hold times are longer than I'd like.

9
00:00:55.246 --> 00:00:58.529
Marcus Bell: Okay. How's PreSense holding up with the longer queues?

10
00:00:58.998 --> 00:01:04.557
Dana Ortiz: Fine. Agents still get the screen pop before they pick up. No complaints there.

11
00:01:04.882 --> 00:01:09.023
Marcus Bell: Good. And the signals side? Appointment booked, quote given, that set.

12
00:01:09.496 --> 00:01:14.301
Priyanka Shah: Working like it always does. We haven't touched the scorecards since July.

13
00:01:14.869 --> 00:01:19.465
Marcus Bell: Alright. Anything you want to put in front of me today?

14
00:01:20.563 --> 00:01:27.427
Dana Ortiz: There is one thing, and it's been nagging at us for a couple of weeks now.

15
00:01:28.069 --> 00:01:28.869
Marcus Bell: Go ahead.

16
00:01:30.122 --> 00:01:38.688
Dana Ortiz: The calls that Invoca sends over to Google Ads as offline conversions, they're landing one to three days after the call.

17
00:01:38.688 --> 00:01:43.990
Dana Ortiz: So Smart Bidding is looking at partial numbers for most of the week.

18
00:01:45.082 --> 00:01:50.665
Marcus Bell: One to three days from when the call happens to when Google shows it?

19
00:01:50.996 --> 00:01:59.526
Dana Ortiz: Right. We can see the call in Invoca, then it's a day or two later before it shows in Google Ads.

20
00:01:59.526 --> 00:02:01.963
Dana Ortiz: Sometimes three if it's a weekend.

21
00:02:03.425 --> 00:02:05.014
Marcus Bell: Who caught it first?

22
00:02:06.045 --> 00:02:14.208
Dana Ortiz: I did, actually. I pull the weekly numbers Monday morning for the leadership call, and our cost per booked job looked off.

23
00:02:14.208 --> 00:02:16.064
Dana Ortiz: Then Priyanka dug into it.

24
00:02:16.394 --> 00:02:27.028
Priyanka Shah: I lined up the export dates against the call dates. About 40 percent of calls from any given day don't show in Google until two days later.

25
00:02:28.437 --> 00:02:33.797
Marcus Bell: And this is the GCLID match, so it's tied to the ad click.

26
00:02:34.555 --> 00:02:39.320
Priyanka Shah: Yeah. The conversions do get there, they just get there late.

27
00:02:40.301 --> 00:02:45.587
Marcus Bell: Got it. Does it affect both the HVAC and the plumbing campaigns?

28
00:02:46.772 --> 00:02:51.592
Dana Ortiz: As far as we can tell, yes. It's not one campaign.

29
00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:54.866
Marcus Bell: Okay. How long has this been going on?

30
00:02:55.096 --> 00:03:02.862
Priyanka Shah: Three weeks at least. I went back through September and it's there too, just less obvious because volumes were lower.

31
00:03:03.522 --> 00:03:14.827
Marcus Bell: Understood. I want to get the exact campaign IDs and a few call examples from you so I can take it to the team with something concrete.

32
00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:16.921
Marcus Bell: Can you pull that today?

33
00:03:17.537 --> 00:03:24.024
Priyanka Shah: Yes. I'll send a sheet with call IDs, timestamps and when each one landed in Google.

34
00:03:25.408 --> 00:03:33.738
Marcus Bell: Perfect. I'm not going to guess at what's causing it from here. I'll open it up and come back to you with what I hear.

35
00:03:34.081 --> 00:03:48.254
Dana Ortiz: That's all I want. We're not changing anything on our end, the bids are what they are, but I can't defend the numbers to our CFO if they're built on half a week of data.

36
00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:53.871
Marcus Bell: Makes sense. Anything else on the Meta side while we're here?

37
00:03:54.795 --> 00:03:56.459
Priyanka Shah: Meta's been quiet. No issues.

38
00:03:57.026 --> 00:04:09.381
Marcus Bell: Okay. One more thing, and this is just scheduling. I'd like to do a quick PreSense review with your contact center leads before the holidays, maybe the first week of November.

39
00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:18.275
Dana Ortiz: That works. I'll get our contact center manager on the invite. She's the one who cares about it most.

40
00:04:18.994 --> 00:04:26.139
Marcus Bell: Great. So, the conversion timing sheet today, and I'll follow up once I've talked to the team.

41
00:04:26.759 --> 00:04:29.413
Priyanka Shah: I'll send it within the hour. Thanks, Marcus.

42
00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:33.064
Marcus Bell: Thanks both. Good luck with furnace season.

43
00:04:33.441 --> 00:04:35.127
Dana Ortiz: We'll need it. Talk soon.
